      "content": "# Transaction Isolation, Race Conditions, and Deadlock Prevention\n\n## 1. Transaction Isolation\n\nTransaction isolation defines how and when the changes made by one transaction become visible to other concurrent transactions. The SQL standard defines four isolation levels, each trading off consistency for concurrency.\n\n### Isolation Levels (from lowest to highest)\n\n| Level | Dirty Reads | Non-Repeatable Reads | Phantom Reads |\n|-------|-------------|----------------------|---------------|\n| Read Uncommitted | \u2705 Possible | \u2705 Possible | \u2705 Possible |\n| Read Committed | \u274c Prevented | \u2705 Possible | \u2705 Possible |\n| Repeatable Read | \u274c Prevented | \u274c Prevented | \u2705 Possible* |\n| Serializable | \u274c Prevented | \u274c Prevented | \u274c Prevented |\n\n*\\*In PostgreSQL, Repeatable Read actually prevents phantom reads via snapshot isolation.*\n\n### Example: Lost Update Problem\n\nConsider two transactions updating a bank account balance:\n\n```sql\n-- Initial balance = 100\n\n-- Transaction A\nBEGIN;\nSELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1;  -- reads 100\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = 100 + 50 WHERE id = 1; ",
